From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: TheDBP <TheDBP@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 17:22:52 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] ether: ImsBridge -- bind the 7.1 ims.apk to the modern
 telephony stack

Presents a modern ImsService and delegates to the legacy IImsService the stock apk exposes, across the
surface the previous patch defines.

The shape difference the bridge absorbs is that 7.1 is serviceId-keyed -- open() returns an int every
later call carries -- while a MMTelFeature instance already implies a session, so the bridge holds the
id.

Direct: startSession/endSession, isConnected, isOpened, addRegistrationListener, createCallProfile,
turnOnIms, turnOffIms, setUiTTYMode. RegistrationListenerAdapter carries ten callbacks straight
across. Parcelable conversion covers ImsCallProfile, ImsReasonInfo and ImsStreamMediaProfile -- each a
handful of instance fields, not the dozens their constant lists suggest.

Six sub-interfaces get a wrapper apiece. CallSessionWrapper delegates all 28 legacy call-session
methods onto ImsCallSessionImplBase, whose no-op defaults cover everything 13 added -- RTT, transfer,
call quality -- that 7.1 has no notion of. CallSessionListenerAdapter carries the 30 callbacks back;
all thirty exist on 13 with matching shape. The session argument resolves to the wrapper already built
rather than a fresh one, so object identity stays stable and the telephony stack can match callbacks
to calls; only the new sessions handed over by merge and conference extension get their own wrapper.

UtWrapper is 16 delegations onto ImsUtImplBase. ConfigWrapper is pure delegation -- 13's IImsConfig is
method-for-method what 7.1 had, and ImsConfigListener likewise.

EcbmWrapper and MultiEndpointWrapper are shaped differently than expected: setListener on those two
ImplBase classes lives on an inner Stub rather than as an overridable, because the base owns the
listener itself and exposes enteredEcbm()/onImsExternalCallStateUpdate() for the implementation to
call. So each registers its own adapter with the legacy service and forwards into the base. The legacy
external-call callback carries a raw List -- AIDL erases the element type -- so entries are checked
rather than cast wholesale.

Three things deliberately do not forward, each logged rather than silently dropped: getVideoCallProvider
returns null (7.1's provider is a different interface, and video is unreachable anyway),
registrationFeatureCapabilityChanged has no 13 equivalent, and removeRegistrationListener cannot be
expressed -- 7.1 offers only setRegistrationListener, which replaces the single listener rather than
detaching one of several.
